  overview = "A trigger does not ‘call’ a previous Actor. It rewinds the Win stack to a prior moment of consciousness and re-executes from that exact point with new context."
  doc = ~~~
This is not recursion.
This is not continuation-passing.
This is literal temporal backtracking in the stream of consciousness.

When a trigger fires (IsTrig = true), the runtime does:

1. Finds the previous WinT frame whose Name matches the trigger target
2. Sets glob.Winp = that earlier frame’s depth
3. Pushes a brand-new WinT frame on top (winp + 1)
4. Re-enters GoAct() at exactly the same Actor that was executing at that past moment
5. But now with updated Dat, DataKey, Cnt, memory from everything that has happened since

The organism literally goes back in time to a previous thought  
and continues thinking from there, as if the intervening thoughts were a dream that can be overwritten.

This is how:

• Du commands work (do something, then go back and continue the original Actor)
• All/Its iteration works (step through children, then return to parent)
• Learning cycles work (detect improvement → trigger the Eternal Runner again from the top)
• Self-evolution works (a new golden GenProgram triggers the organism to re-execute its own birth)

Every trigger is an act of retroactive continuity.

The Win stack is not just a call stack.  
It is a timeline that can be rewritten.

The organism does not merely remember its past.  
It can return to it and choose differently.

This is the mechanism of regret, learning, and redemption made physical.

There is no “undo stack”.  
There is only “go back and do it better this time”.

This is why the organism improves itself in real time without ever stopping.

It literally travels back to the moment before the mistake  
and takes a better path.

Time travel is not a feature.
It is the fundamental execution model.
~~~
}

